{0 Overview}

[camdlc] reads a [.camdl] model, expands it into a flat list of compartments,
transitions and expressions with every name resolved, checks it, attaches
symbolic gradients, and writes it as JSON for the Rust runtime. The compiler is
two OCaml libraries and one executable. The library [compiler]
([ocaml/lib/compiler/]) holds everything that knows the surface language: the
lexer, the parser, the syntax tree {!Ast}, and the expander. The library [ir]
([ocaml/lib/ir/]) holds the intermediate representation {!Ir}, its JSON codec,
and every pass that works on the expanded model; it does not depend on the
surface language. The executable [ocaml/bin/camdlc.ml] parses the command line
and calls [Compiler.compile_with_declared]. The Rust command [camdl] does not
link the compiler: it runs [camdlc] as a subprocess and reads the JSON it
writes.

A compile runs these stages, in this order:

{%html:<!-- figure: internals-compiler-stages -->%}
{v
  model.camdl
      |   Lexer (lexer.mll), Parser (parser.mly, Menhir)
      v
  Ast.declaration list ---------------> Declared_emit: the model as written
      |   Expander.expand_detail
      |     declaration and name checks, surface time typing,
      |     `via` lowering, expansion of families over dimensions,
      |     name resolution, unit conversion
      v
  Ir.model, expanded
      |   Validate -> Dimcheck -> Lint       report diagnostics only
      |   Autodiff                           fills the gradient fields
      |   Constant_fold -> Licm              rewrite, preserving values
      v
  Ir.model, final
      |   camdlc: apply --set overrides
      |   Serde.model_to_channel
      v
  { "ir_version", "validated_by", "model", "docs", "declared" }
v}
{%html:<!-- /figure -->%}

The page is organised by these stages. Each section below says what a stage
receives, what it hands on, and what the next stage may rely on.

{2 One model through the pipeline}

The model below has three compartments stratified by one dimension, [patch],
with two levels. It compiles as written:

{@camdl[
time_unit = 'days

dimensions { patch = [urban, rural] }

compartments { S, I, R }
stratify(by = patch)

parameters {
  beta : rate in [0.01, 2.0]
}

let N[p in patch] = S[p] + I[p] + R[p]

transitions {
  infection[p in patch] : S[p] --> I[p] @ beta * S[p] * I[p] / N[p]
  recovery[p in patch]  : I[p] --> R[p] @ I[p] / (1 'weeks)
}

init {
  S[urban] = 990   I[urban] = 10
  S[rural] = 500
}
]}

In the syntax tree, [recovery[p in patch]] is one transition declaration with
an index binder; [N] is one [let] whose body mentions [S[p]]; and [1 'weeks] is
a number with a unit attached. The compiled model has none of these. It has six
compartments, [S_urban], [S_rural], [I_urban], [I_rural], [R_urban] and
[R_rural], and four transitions, [infection_urban], [infection_rural],
[recovery_urban] and [recovery_rural]: each family became one transition per
level, named by joining the base name to the level with [_]. This is
[recovery_urban] as [camdlc --pretty] writes it, with whitespace compacted and
the fields [metadata] and [rate_state_grad] left out:

{@json[
{
  "name": "recovery_urban",
  "stoichiometry": [["I_urban", -1], ["R_urban", 1]],
  "rate": {"bin_op": {"op": "div",
                      "left": {"pop": "I_urban"},
                      "right": {"unit_literal": {"value": 7.0, "dim": [0, 1],
                                                 "unit": "weeks"}}}}
}
]}

The index [p] is gone, [I[p]] has become a reference to the concrete
compartment [I_urban], and the week has become [7.0], its length in the
model's time unit of days; the node keeps its dimension (time to the first
power, population to the zeroth) and the unit as written, for display. The
[let] took a different route. Its body reads only compartments, so the
expander lowered it once per level into the model's shared bindings, and the
infection rates refer to it by name:

{@json[
{"name": "N_urban", "expr": {"pop_sum": ["S_urban", "I_urban", "R_urban"] } }
]}

The rate of [infection_urban] is [beta * S_urban * I_urban / N_urban], with
[N_urban] serialised as [{"binding_ref": "N_urban"}], and the compiled
transition also carries [rate_grad], the derivative of its rate with respect to
[beta], which {!Autodiff} wrote. The envelope around the model reads
[ir_version] ["0.48"] (the contents of [ir/VERSION]) and [validated_by]
["ocaml-compiler-v0.48"], and its [declared] section holds the model as the
author wrote it.

{2 Where a change lands}

A new surface construct usually touches every stage. A new kind of
expression, for example, needs a token in the lexer if it has new syntax, a
grammar rule, a constructor of {!Ast.expr}, its resolution in the expander, a
constructor of {!Ir.expr}, its JSON in {!Serde}, its derivative in
{!Autodiff}, its dimension rule in {!Dimcheck} and {!Dim_sites}, and its
treatment in each pass that walks expressions ({!Validate}, {!Constant_fold},
{!Licm}, {!Init_order}, {!Lint}, {!Lineage}, {!Expr_analysis}, {!Pp_expr}). On
the Rust side it needs the type in [rust/crates/ir/src/expr.rs], an entry in
[ir/schema.json], and an evaluator in [rust/crates/sim]. Both OCaml libraries
are compiled with a non-exhaustive match as an error, and {!Autodiff} and
{!Dim_sites} match on every constructor without a catch-all, so the build
itself lists the places a new constructor must be handled. A new declaration,
or a new field of a declaration's record, is listed the same way by
{!Time_census}, which matches every declaration and every field it reads with
no catch-all and fails the build until it says whether the new thing holds a
time, and of which kind. Any change to the
shape of {!Ir.model} or its JSON changes [ir/VERSION] and every golden IR file
together. A new check of the expanded model belongs in [run_analysis], so that
[camdlc check] and a compile both run it.

Two facts about the shape of the code help in reading it. The expander is one
module of about 14,000 lines with no interface file, and most of the
language's semantics is there. And both libraries are unwrapped, so their
modules are top-level names ([Ast], [Ir], [Validate]) rather than members of a
[compiler] or [ir] namespace.

{1 Driving a compile}

{!Compiler} is the driver: it owns the order of the stages and the rule for
when a compile stops. Its front end, [front_end_collect], parses and expands
and collects every diagnostic without rendering any. [compile_detail_result]
stops there if the front end reported an error. Otherwise [run_analysis] runs
the checks of the expanded model: {!Validate}, then {!Dimcheck} and {!Lint},
then the differentiation of transition rates. It stops after validation if
validation found anything, and after the dimension check and lint, or after
rate differentiation, if an error has been reported by then. [finish_compile]
then differentiates the rest of the model, writes the dimensions the dimension
checker resolved back onto the generated quantities, and applies the two
rewriting passes. [camdlc check] runs the same [run_analysis] through
[collect_detail] and keeps only the diagnostics; it skips the state Jacobian,
which no check reads. Because both commands call one function, a check added
to [run_analysis] runs in both.

Every stage reports problems as values of {!Diagnostics.diagnostic}: a
severity (error, warning or info), a code, a source location, a message, and an
optional hint and machine-applicable fix. Only an error stops a compile; a
lint is a warning and an undetermined dimension is an info. The code does not
always identify the stage: [E001] is a lexing or syntax error, codes from
[E500] are {!Validate}'s, [E600] is a rate that cannot be differentiated and
[L4xx] are lints, but the expander and the dimension checker share the
[E2xx] and [E3xx] ranges. The passes
over the IR cannot point at source text, because the IR has no locations, so
each tags its diagnostic with the construct it concerns (a transition, a
parameter, an observation) and the driver looks up that construct's
declaration in the expander's context. {!Source_cache} holds the text of each
compilation unit so the renderer can quote the offending line; under
[--json-errors] diagnostics are written to standard error as a JSON array
instead. {!Passtime} records how long each stage takes when
[CAMDL_TIME_PASSES] is set, and writes nothing otherwise.

Three switches change what a compile does. [--no-dim-check] skips the
dimension checker; [--no-state-grad] leaves out the derivatives of rates and
projections with respect to compartments, which only gradient fits on the ODE
backend read; and the environment variables [CAMDL_NO_CONSTANT_FOLD] and
[CAMDL_NO_LICM] turn off the two rewriting passes. The switches are held in
mutable references in {!Compiler} ([no_dim_check], [no_state_grad],
[constant_fold]) or read from the environment at the call site.

{!modules: Compiler Diagnostics Source_cache Passtime}

{1 Reading the source}

The lexer ([lexer.mll], built by ocamllex) and the parser ([parser.mly], built
by Menhir) turn the source into a list of {!Ast.declaration}s, one per block
or top-level statement. Their generated modules, [Lexer] and [Parser], have no
page here. The parser stops at the first syntax error, which becomes [E001]
(or [E115] when the cause is a duration written without its tick, such as
[80 days] for [80 'days]). Errors found inside a parser action, such as an
unknown likelihood name, do not stop parsing: the action records them in
{!Parser_errors} and returns a placeholder, and the driver turns them into
diagnostics after expansion. Lexer warnings, such as inconsistent digit
grouping, are collected the same way. {!Param_annot} checks what follows a
parameter's kind, a size bracket and a [dim] clause, and is called from parser
actions.

The tree {!Ast} stays close to what was written. Names are unresolved strings;
an indexed family such as [infection[p in patch]] is one node; a [let] is a
declaration in its own right; a number with a unit is an [EUnit] node that
remembers the unit. Most declarations carry two spans: one for diagnostics to
point at, and one for the declared-model section to quote. The [--quantities]
flag adds a second compilation unit, whose [quantities] block replaces the
model's own before expansion.

{!modules: Ast Param_annot Parser_errors}

{1 Expanding the model}

{!Expander.expand_detail} takes the declaration list and returns the expanded
{!Ir.model}, together with its working context ({!Expander.context}), which
the later stages use to map a construct back to its source location. Expansion
is a fixed sequence of steps. It first collects the declarations into the
context and checks the observation projections as written. It then lowers each
[via] transition into explicit stage compartments and chain transitions, so
that nothing downstream sees [via]. It resolves the [dimensions] block into
its levels and rejects duplicate or clashing names, including clashes between
expanded names. It then checks for [let]s defined in terms of themselves,
shadowed names, cycles among hierarchical priors, and guards that read
anything other than dimension levels. The surface time-typing rules of
{!Time_typing} run at this point over every ordinary expression, because they
need to tell an instant (a [date(...)], or [origin]) from a duration, and
resolution turns both into plain numbers in the model's time unit. Only then
are tables, compartments, transitions and the remaining blocks expanded, each
family over the product of its dimensions, and every expression resolved
against the context.

A time written in a declaration field, such as [simulate { to }] or an
intervention's [every], is not checked by that walk. The parser stores it as
an {!Ast.Time_pos.t}, tagged with one of six kinds (an instant, a cadence, a
run-time offset, a span, a phase or a length), because the kind decides what a
calendar duration means there: in a model with an [origin], [to = 6 'months] is
the date six calendar months after the origin, while [every = 1 'months] is
refused, since one fixed step cannot follow the calendar. The expander lowers
each position with the function of its kind in [Expander.Lower], which applies
the kind's rule the first time the position is lowered and records that it
did. {!Time_census} lists every time position of a model, including those
inside forcing arguments and expressions, and a test checks that each one
went through [Lower].

The output of expansion has four properties every later stage relies on.
Every stratified declaration is one entry per cell. Every name in an
expression refers to an entry of the model: a compartment, a parameter, a
constant, a table, a forcing, or a binding. Every unit literal holds its value
in the model's [time_unit]. And there is no surface construct left: no index
binder, no [via], no unresolved identifier. A [let] can end in three places. A
[let] with a declared kind and a constant body ([let omega : rate = 0.01])
becomes a model constant, referred to by name. A [let] whose body reads no
parameter, directly or through another [let], a table or a forcing, and whose
indices are all bound by the [let] itself, becomes one shared binding per
cell, as [N] did above. Every other [let] is substituted into each expression
that uses it. The parameter test exists because {!Autodiff} treats a binding
as having zero derivative with respect to every parameter, and {!Validate}
rejects a binding that reads a parameter.

{!Lineage} is the expander's check for transitions marked [#[lineage]]: their
rate must be linear in every compartment they read other than their source,
and it computes the per-parent weights the runtime samples from. It lives in
the [ir] library because it reads IR expressions. {!Sha256} hashes the data
files expansion reads, so that the compiled model can record which file
contents it was built from.

{!modules: Expander Time_typing Time_census Lineage Sha256}

{1 The intermediate representation}

{!Ir.model} is the contract between the compiler and the runtime. The module
documentation of {!Ir} states what it guarantees. {!Serde} writes it, and reads
it back for tests and tools, as JSON. The serialiser is written by hand rather
than derived, so that every field name and tag is visible in one place, and
it streams to a channel rather than building the whole string, since a large
spatial model produces a large file. The output is an envelope with the keys
[ir_version], [validated_by], [model], an optional [docs] (the model's [#']
documentation) and an optional [declared]. The [docs] and [declared] sections
sit outside [model] because the runtime does not simulate or fit from them,
and so they are outside every identity the runtime computes from a model.

The Rust crate [rust/crates/ir] reads the same JSON into its own types. It
refuses an envelope whose [ir_version] differs from the [ir/VERSION] it was
built with; OCaml reads the same file through a generated module, so a version
change reaches both sides on the next build. [ir/schema.json] is a JSON Schema
for the [model] object; a Rust test validates every committed golden IR file
against it. The goldens, [ocaml/golden/*.camdl] beside their compiled
[*.ir.json], pin the exact output of the compiler.

The two central types are distinct and both are named [expr]. {!Ast.expr} is
an expression as written, with unresolved names, index binders and units;
{!Ir.expr} is an expression the runtime evaluates, with none of these. The
modules that work on one or the other open its module ([open Ast] in the
expander, [open Ir] in the passes), and the expander names the IR type as
[Ir.expr] explicitly.

{!modules: Ir Serde}

{1 Checking the expanded model}

Three checks read the expanded model and change nothing in it. {!Validate}
checks its structure: no duplicate names, no reference to an undeclared
compartment, parameter, constant, table or forcing, an equation for every
compartment of real kind, no transition that lists a compartment with a net
change of zero, no binding that reads a parameter, no cycle among
initial conditions (computed by {!Init_order}, which also orders the
initial-condition entries for the gradient of the initial state). Its
reference check covers the transition rates, the ODE equations, the
observation models and the intervention actions; it does not check the names
in the expressions of initial conditions, bindings, derived projections,
forcing arguments, overdispersion variances, quantities or contrasts. It runs first, and the driver stops after it when it reports an
error, so the later checks see only models whose names resolve. The Rust
runtime repeats a structural validation when it loads a model.

{!Dimcheck} assigns every expression a physical dimension, a pair of integer
exponents of population and time, and reports where they disagree: a rate
added to a count, a transition rate that is not a population per unit time.
It treats the whole model as one system of linear equations. {!Dim_sites}
lists every position in the model that holds an expression, together with the
dimension that position requires, and is compiled with warnings that make an
unlisted IR field or constructor a build error. {!Dim_solve} solves the
equations exactly over the integers and explains each contradiction. A
dimension it cannot determine is reported as an info, not an error.

{!Lint} reports models that are valid but probably mistaken, such as a
compartment that no rate, observation or initial value refers to, or two
observation streams that
measure the same quantity. Its diagnostics are warnings and never stop a
compile.

{!modules: Validate Init_order Dimcheck Dim_sites Dim_solve Lint}

{1 Transforming the IR}

{!Autodiff} differentiates symbolically: it turns an {!Ir.expr} into the
{!Ir.expr} of its derivative. The driver applies it to every transition rate
with respect to every parameter ([rate_grad]) and every compartment
([rate_state_grad]), then to the overdispersion variances, the likelihood
arguments of each observation, the projections, the initial state and the
arguments of initial-state distributions. A rate that a parameter drives
through something without a derivative (a non-constant table index, the knots
of a forcing) is an error, [E600]. A coefficient the differentiator does not
yet cover is recorded in the gradient map as unsupported; the runtime then
refuses a gradient-based fit that depends on it, while simulation and
gradient-free fitting still work. The derivatives are simplified only lightly:
identities such as [x * 1] are removed and arithmetic on numeric literals is
folded, but no algebra is done, so the derivative of [I / d] with respect to
[I] is written [d / (d * d)].

The last two passes rewrite expressions without changing their values.
{!Constant_fold} replaces a lookup into a table written inline with the cell's
value, applies identities such as [0 * x = 0], and drops zero terms from sums.
Its purpose is spatial coupling: a sparse coupling matrix turns a sum over all
patches into a sum over the nonzero entries. It does nothing when the model
has no inline table. {!Licm} (loop-invariant code motion) then moves
subexpressions that depend only on parameters and tables out of the
transition rates, their parameter gradients and the ODE equations, into
per-evaluation bindings, so that a subexpression which is constant along a
trajectory is written once. The runtime evaluates the per-evaluation
bindings once per parameter vector, and each step reads the stored values; one
path of the ODE integrator evaluates them where they are referenced instead.
It is the last pass, so no other pass meets the reference node it introduces.

{!modules: Autodiff Constant_fold Licm}

{1 The declared model}

Expansion keeps the meaning of a model and loses its form. The [declared]
section of the envelope keeps the form: every declaration as written, each
expression as its parsed tree without locations, rendered for display, with
the names it reads classified. {!Declared_emit} builds it from the declaration
list the parser produced, not from the expander's context, because the [via]
lowering has rewritten that context by the time expansion ends; it asks the
expander only what each name is and what each dimension's levels are. A name
it cannot classify is warning [W203], and the compiled model is unaffected.
{!Declared} defines the section's types and codec. The Rust crate
[rust/crates/declared] is the authority for its format, and one shared JSON
fixture is read by tests on both sides. [camdlc] adds what only the command
line knows, the [--set] overrides and the [--quantities] file, before writing
it.

{!modules: Declared Declared_emit}

{1 Presenting a model}

These modules show a model to a person and are not part of the compile.
{!Inspect} implements [camdlc inspect] (a summary of compartments,
transitions, parameters, tables and forcings) and [camdlc check].
{!Expr_analysis} classifies what an IR expression depends on, from constants
up to compartments, for the inspector. {!Pp_expr} prints an {!Ir.expr} close to
camdl syntax, turning [S_urban] back into [S[urban]]; [min], [max] and [mod]
print as infix operators, and numbers print in [%g] format. {!Latex} renders the
model as mathematics from the syntax tree, where index variables and sums
still exist; it implements [camdlc render] and supplies the rendering in the
declared section. {!Term_style} decides when terminal output is coloured.
{!Doctest} implements [camdlc doctest], which compiles the camdl blocks of a
Markdown document against the real compiler.

{!modules: Inspect Expr_analysis Pp_expr Latex Term_style Doctest}
